// Lesson 3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy
// SOLO BUILD (scaffold level 4). This is the lesson the course has been
// building toward: everything a projectile needs has already been done once,
// somewhere else, wearing different clothes. So nothing here is given — the
// student splits the problem, looks each part up, and writes the script.
//
// GROUND TRUTH: Projectile.cs is an empty stub but IS already attached to
// Turret_Projectile.prefab. That prefab has NO Rigidbody2D and NO Collider2D,
// so any physics-based movement option costs the student an added component.
// Turret.prefab has a child "SpawnPoint" that serves as the muzzle.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass, "teach it once"): one editor block, one code block. The
// wiring and the first run are the last steps of the same editor list, marked
// "Once the code half is done". Culled as repetition: the paragraph introducing
// each half, and the six-item reread list — its lessons are already linked from
// the decompose block, the resources block and the steps themselves.
//
// The parts below are NOT rendered at level 4 (see src/lib/decompose.ts) — they
// are the record of the intended split. Do not delete them as "unused".
//
// 3.2 had no socratic block at all, so sc1 is new: three questions at the end of
// the code half. The decompose block keeps its single turn (dc1:q2).
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'A turret that shoots at me. That is the whole feature, and it is far too big to hold ' +
    'in your head at once — which is exactly why every lesson in this course starts the same way.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Notice the player is close enough',
      does: 'Compare the distance between turret and player against a radius, every frame.',
      doc: 'You already wrote this in 3.1 — Vector3.Distance(a, b) and a range check in Update().',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Stamp out a copy of the bullet',
      does: 'Clone the projectile prefab into the scene at the muzzle, at the moment of firing.',
      doc: 'Instantiate(Object original, Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation), met in 2.4 when you spawned pickups.',
      href: '2.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Tell that copy where to go',
      does: 'Hand the clone a direction, computed once, from the muzzle toward where the player was.',
      doc: 'Vector subtraction, then .normalized — the same pair you used for the diagonal fix in 1.5.',
      href: '1.5.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move it, every frame',
      does: 'Advance the projectile along its stored direction at a speed, without depending on frame rate.',
      doc: 'transform.Translate(Vector3 translation) from 1.4 with deltaTime, or your movement method from 2.2. The trade-off is yours to make.',
      href: '2.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Get rid of it',
      does: 'Schedule the clone to destroy itself after a few seconds so misses do not accumulate.',
      doc: 'Destroy(Object obj) — the same call that made a coin vanish in 2.1.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Notice what is NOT on that list: no new API except the spawner, and even that you have ' +
    'seen. Five parts, four of them lifted straight out of lessons you have already finished. ' +
    'That is what a well-sequenced course feels like from the inside — not "here is a new ' +
    'thing you have never seen", but "here is the same five things, pointed at a new target".',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the turret shoot" cannot be looked up. "Clone a prefab at a position" can. ' +
    'If a part is still too big to search for, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'There is more than one way to make the bullet travel. Name two, say what each one costs you in components, and say which you picked and why.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret knows when you are near; now it needs teeth. Everything a projectile needs, you
    have already written once, somewhere else. Your job today is to recognise that, and then prove
    it — no API table this time, two files, and neither of them new.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I shoot a projectile?', 4)}

${renderDecompose('3.2', DECOMPOSE, 4)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> an empty <strong>Projectile</strong> component and nothing
            else — no collider, no body. Whatever your bullet needs to move, you add it yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> a <strong>Turret</strong> instance in the
            <strong>Overworld</strong>, then <strong>Inspector → Turret Controller</strong>: two
            slots sit empty, and they fill up in step 3. Expand the turret and click the child
            <strong>SpawnPoint</strong> — that is the muzzle, not the turret's centre and not the
            world origin.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Inspector → Turret Controller →</strong> drag
            <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code> from the <strong>Project window</strong>
            into the prefab slot, and the <strong>Hierarchy's</strong> SpawnPoint into the muzzle
            slot. Do both turrets. An empty slot says nothing at startup and throws the instant you
            step into range, which looks like a firing bug and is a setup bug.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and walk into range: a projectile appears at the
            muzzle and travels. Do not celebrate the fire rate — you are firing every frame, because
            the range check runs every frame, and that is the next lesson's problem.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What the finished feature does, from the player's seat:</strong></p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            Something appears at the <strong>end of the barrel</strong> — not at the turret's centre,
            and not at the world origin.
        </li>
        <li>
            It flies toward <strong>where I was standing when it was fired</strong>. If I keep
            moving, it does not follow me: it commits to the shot it took.
        </li>
        <li>
            It travels at <strong>the same speed</strong> whether I am against the turret or at the
            far edge of its range.
        </li>
        <li>
            It <strong>cleans itself up</strong> a few seconds later if it hits nothing.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural constraint (the only thing I will hand you):</strong> you are editing
        two files and neither knows the other's details. <code>TurretController.cs</code> decides
        <em>when</em> to fire and <em>where</em> the bullet starts. <code>Projectile.cs</code> owns
        everything after that — which way it goes, how fast, and when it dies. If you find yourself
        writing the bullet's speed into the turret, stop: the turret should not know that number
        exists.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Two Files, One Shot</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The turret fires from its muzzle rather than from its centre. Which of your parts does that change, and what would a bullet spawned at the turret's centre look like to the player?</li>
<li>The bullet commits to the direction it was fired in and does not follow you. Which line of your plan makes that true, and what would you have to add to make it home in on the player instead?</li>
<li>To turn this into a shotgun that fires five bullets in a spread, which of the two files do you rewrite — and what does your answer prove about why the split is worth having?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break a feature into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say where you reread the part you lifted from an earlier lesson</li>
        <li>Explain why the bullet starts at the muzzle and commits to one direction instead of following the player</li>
        <li>Explain why a direction vector is normalized, and what goes wrong without it</li>
        <li>Name two ways to move a projectile, state what each one costs you in components, and explain why your turret currently fires every frame</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the projectile lifecycle and aiming in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the clone call, the destroy call, and Mathf</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.3.html">3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</a>, where you fix the bullet hose you just built.</p>
</div>`;
